// @no-instantiate
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: `std:array`'s generic exports over a LITERAL-UNION element emit
// a module the engine refuses. This is the worst of the three answers `std:array`'s
// generic surface gives (the module header enumerates them), and it is worth a fixture
// precisely because it is NOT an error: `vl check` exits 0 with no diagnostic at any
// severity, `vl build` writes the `.wasm` file and reports success, and the failure
// arrives only when something tries to instantiate it —
//
//   type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found i32
//
// PRE-EXISTING, and not `sorted`'s doing. The same program with `indexOf`,
// `lastIndexOf`, `includes`, `count`, `reduce` or `mapIndexed` produces the identical
// invalid module, as does a hand-written `function pick<T>(self: T[], f: (T, T) =>
// boolean)` with no std involved. A NUMERIC literal union (`(1 | 2)[]`) is refused
// earlier, at emit, even by `reverse`.
//
// THE HEADER'S DIVIDING LINE WAS WRONG, AND RE-DERIVING IT MOVES THE DEFECT OUT OF STD
// ENTIRELY. It said the trigger was a SIGNATURE that puts `T` in a CLOSURE parameter or
// compares two `T`s with `==`. It is neither. Four probes, each one strictly smaller than
// the last:
//
//   · no closure — `function pick<T>(self: T[]): T { self[0] }` over `("a" | "b")[]`
//     emits `(param (ref $atomArray)) (result (ref $string))` and does not validate;
//   · no generic either — `function pick(self: ("a" | "b")[]): "a" | "b" { self[0] }`,
//     a plain function, emits exactly the same mismatch;
//   · no function at all — `const v: "a" | "b" = tags[0]` beside
//     `const tags: ("a" | "b")[]` compiles, VALIDATES, runs, and prints the raw atom id
//     `0`. That is a SILENTLY WRONG VALUE, a strictly worse tier than this file;
//   · every one of those with the DECLARED alias `type K = "a" | "b"` in place of the
//     inline spelling is correct, this fixture's own program included.
//
// So the trigger is the INLINE literal-union SPELLING meeting a value that came out of
// ELEMENT position, and std, generics and closures are all incidental. `ctxKeepsLitUnion`
// preserves a litunion's members at `RC_ELEM` and softens them at `RC_ROOT` /
// `RC_FN_PARAM`, so `("a" | "b")[]` is an array of interned i32 ATOMS while `"a" | "b"`
// in any other position is a string reference — and the two meet at every read of an
// element. Its own header states the policy, names the two candidate position rules that
// were built and REFUTED, and says why the spelling has to be position-INDEPENDENT: the
// union registry is keyed by NAME and blind to position, so a rule that spells one arena
// type two ways mints two rows for one type.
//
// SO THIS IS A FAMILY DECISION, NOT A MISSING ARM — the same shape as
// `soundness/xfail-miscompile-numeric-litunion-empty-list-seed.vl`. Closing it means
// choosing "preserve everywhere" and giving the two valtype ladders the INLINE
// member-spelling arm they lack (`ctxKeepsLitUnion`'s header names the four cells that go
// red without it), or choosing "soften everywhere" and losing the atom rep for
// `("a" | "b")[]`. It is filed with its exact diff in
// `docs/internals/destringify-types-program.md` (2026-07-29).
//
// IT IS NOT THE `$fnsig` SPELLING-KEY DEFECT it was grouped with in #1863. That one
// (`soundness/xfail-miscompile-narrowed-litunion-fn-value-arg.vl`) is CLOSED and now lives
// at `tests/cases/literal-unions/narrowed-litunion-fn-value-arg.vl`; closing it changed
// nothing here, which is the measurement. The disassembly says why the two are unrelated:
// here the interned `$fnsig`, the comparator lambda's own functype and the call site all
// AGREE that the parameter is a string — the odd one out is the ARRAY, whose backing is
// `(array (mut i32))`.
//
// HOW IT GOT INTO THE HEADER AS "WORKS". The reach paragraph in `std/array.vl` first
// claimed literal unions were admitted, on the strength of a probe that used
// `reverse` — the one shape-blind export in the module. Probing a generic surface with
// its most permissive arm measures the arm, not the surface; the header now says so
// and this file is the witness.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED, this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the
// point. The fix is to delete it and fold the literal-union case into
// `array-sorted.vl` beside the `string` receiver, and to correct the header's
// enumeration — noting that the ALIAS spelling of this very program already works, so
// what `array-sorted.vl` gains is the INLINE row, not the family.
import { sorted } from "std:array"

const tags: ("a" | "b")[] = ["b", "a", "b"]
print(tags.sorted((x: "a" | "b", y: "a" | "b") => x < y)[0])
